  "query_preview": "in through the Tri-Cities and Thumb. Loss of diurnal heating\nand anemic moisture in the mid-levels will continue to erode cloud \ncover through the evening. This will lead to clear skies overnight \nand will allow temperatures to quickly drop into the upper 30s for \nportions of the northern Tri- Cities and interior of the Thumb, and \nlow to mid 40s elsewhere. Additionally, north to north-east flow \nbrings a low chance for stratus development by the mid-morning hours \ngiven this lake influence, most favorable across the Thumb. \n\nSurface high pressure will be centered over southern Michigan \ntomorrow morning and will depart into the continental northeast by \ntomorrow evening. This will bring dry weather with some afternoon cu \ndevelopment along with slightly below normal temperatures to near \nnormal temperatures with highs in the low to mid 60s. Generally \nbenign conditions exist for the remainder of the forecast window. \nThere will be some lower-end rain chances centered Sunday morning as \na mid-level wave travels from the northern Plains into the Great \nLakes. This will bring the chance for brief and light rain showers \nmost favorable north of I-94. High pressure to then fill in behind \nthe wave on Monday and Tuesday, bringing dry conditions under mostly \nsunny skies. Outside of a very weak signal for a second chance for \nbrief light rain showers late Wednesday into early Thursday morning \nwith the arrival of a second glancing mid-level wave, very limited \nforcing provides very little chances for any meaningful \nprecipitation chances at least through the middle of next week.",
